  "worked_solution": "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{\u2032}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{\u2032}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=({x}^{3}+4x-6)^{\u2032}=3{x}^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}-6}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}+4)-({x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}-6)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{0}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.3)^{3}+6}{3(1.3)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.145\u2026\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{1}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.145\u2026)^{3}+6}{3(1.145\u2026)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.134\u2026, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{2}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.134\u2026)^{3}+6}{3(1.134\u2026)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.134\u2026.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}\u22481.13$ and ${x}_{3}\u22481.13$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x\u22481.13,$ rounded to two decimal places.",
